00:29.000 --> 00:37.000
Alright, so, we use volume imbalance for our entries, okay, most of the time.

00:37.000 --> 00:44.000
And we can also use them as support and resistance levels, institutional support and resistance levels.

00:44.000 --> 00:48.000
Now, what is a volume imbalance?

00:48.000 --> 00:54.000
It is a difference between a close and the open.

00:54.000 --> 00:58.000
It is a gap between the close and the open of the candle.

00:58.000 --> 01:05.000
So, in this example, you can see this candle right here, it closed right here.

01:05.000 --> 01:19.000
And the next candle, instead of opening at the closing price, it opens below the closing price, leaving this imbalance or volume imbalance around here.

01:20.000 --> 01:26.000
Now, this is a two-candle pattern, okay, so...

01:32.000 --> 01:36.000
So, this volume imbalance occurs between two candles, okay?

01:36.000 --> 01:48.000
So, you can see right here, the candle closed here, and the next candle, instead of opening at the closing price of the previous candle, it opens just below it, right?

01:48.000 --> 01:53.000
So, there was so much volume that the candle just, you know, opened here.

01:53.000 --> 02:08.000
So, the gap right from the close to the open is what you call volume imbalance, okay, sorry for that.

02:08.000 --> 02:21.000
And the, over here for the long example, the difference or the gap between the close and the open of the next candle is called volume imbalance.

02:21.000 --> 02:35.000
I'm going to repeat this again, you can see this down candle closed here, and normally what would happen is the next candle would open from the closing price of the previous candle.

02:35.000 --> 02:58.000
But when this happens, the next candle opens below the closing price of the previous candle, or above the closing price of the previous candle, the gap between the opening and close is what you call volume imbalance, okay?

02:58.000 --> 03:05.000
So, in the future, if price comes back into this level, you can look for long positions, okay?

03:05.000 --> 03:15.000
And similarly for this, sorry, this is short positions, so you can look too shorted, okay?

03:15.000 --> 03:23.000
And, yeah, you can long from here as well, because this X has a resistance and support level.

03:23.000 --> 03:31.000
So, if the volume imbalance is broken, that if the support resistance level is broken, it's going to turn into support, right?

03:31.000 --> 03:43.000
So, let's say price comes back, gives a small reaction, pushes up, and then you can take this volume imbalance as a support level, then you can look for longs, just like support and resistance, okay?

03:43.000 --> 03:53.000
But these are very stronger than support and resistance, normal support and resistance, and sometimes I even find them more powerful than your value gap.

